 The web app keeps sessions in Postgres ([keep sessions in the database](adr/0001-keep-sessions-in-the-database.md))
 and queues jobs there for the worker. A confirmed pantry order is captured through the card gateway by
 `larder/capture.py`.
+
+## Before adding a service
+
+For whoever proposes another database, queue, search engine or other service. Start a proposal from this rule:
+larder runs one database and one web app. A new service we operate must do something Postgres cannot, and at least
+two volunteers must be able to restore it. A proposal needs to explain both the capability Postgres cannot provide
+and who those two volunteers are.
+
+Every service adds work for the volunteers who run larder. If only one person can restore it, an outage depends on
+that person's availability. Keeping sessions and jobs in Postgres reuses the database we already run and back up.
+
+The decisions behind this rule are:
+
+- In 2023, Redis for sessions was rejected because only one volunteer would have run it. Sessions went into
+  Postgres; see [keep sessions in the database](adr/0001-keep-sessions-in-the-database.md).
+- In March 2025, Redis holding Celery's queue evicted 300 receipt jobs. Only one volunteer knew how to restore it.
+  Jobs moved to Postgres; see [run background jobs on Postgres](adr/0002-run-background-jobs-on-postgres.md).
+- In [issue 70](../notes/issue-70.md), Elasticsearch was proposed for slow pantry item search that missed
+  "Rice, brown" when searching for "rice". Postgres full-text search on the item name found the item and reduced
+  the reported search time from four seconds to 40 ms, so the proposal moved to Postgres instead.
+
+This rule allows outside services: the card gateway is already external, and a hosted service that no volunteer
+has to operate is an acceptable option. It does not ban a new service we operate when Postgres cannot meet the
+need and two volunteers can restore it.
